{"parentUuid":"45f1dbd5-0936-45ec-a8f9-1d976ec9ece4","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"49832040-465b-48c3-8853-fc9aaaff2d1f","agentId":"a4438d81d0b74ba38","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XhGvj6MdY79Ht1wyTMeGYs","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"SAT 2024 conference accepted papers LBD clause learning preprocessing\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"VLDB 2024 | Awesome Papers\",\"url\":\"https://paper.lingyunyang.com/reading-notes/conference/vldb-2024\"},{\"title\":\"Predicting Learnt Clauses Quality in Modern SAT Solvers∗ Gilles Audemard\",\"url\":\"https://www.ijcai.org/Proceedings/09/Papers/074.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Effective preprocessing in SAT through variable and clause elimination | Proceedings of the 8th international conference on Theory and Applications of Satisfiability Testing\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1007/11499107_5\"},{\"title\":\"Streamlining Distributed SAT Solver Design - DROPS\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.SAT.2025.27\"},{\"title\":\"dblp: SAT 2024\",\"url\":\"https://dblp.org/db/conf/sat/sat2024.html\"},{\"title\":\"Community and LBD-Based Clause Sharing Policy for Parallel SAT Solving | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-51825-7_2\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - azminewasi/Awesome-LLMs-ICLR-24: It is a comprehensive resource hub compiling all LLM papers accepted at the International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR) in 2024. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/azminewasi/Awesome-LLMs-ICLR-24\"},{\"title\":\"Learn to Unlearn\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.SAT.2025.14\"},{\"title\":\"Rethinking Clause Management for CDCL SAT Solvers\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2602.20829\"},{\"title\":\"SAT 2024 | Call for Papers\",\"url\":\"https://satisfiability.org/SAT24/cfp.php\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about SAT 2024 conference papers. Here's what I discovered:\n\n## SAT 2024 Conference Overview\n\nSAT 2024 was the 27th International Conference on Theory and Applications of Satisfiability Testing, held in Pune, India, August 21-24, 2024.\n\n## Papers Related to Your Query Topics\n\nThe conference included papers related to clause learning and preprocessing:\n\n**Preprocessing and Clause Handling:**\nOne paper discussed replacing redundant pre- and inprocessing at all threads with single-core preprocessing that runs next to parallel search, and removing LBD values from the clause-sharing operation.\n\n**LBD-Based Clause Quality:**\nPapers addressed identifying high-quality clauses using metrics including clause length, literal block distance (LBD), and clause usage in propagation, with proposals for clause-sharing policies based on a combination of LBD and community structure of Boolean formulas.\n\n**Clause Elimination and Learning:**\nThe conference included a paper on \"Dynamic Blocked Clause Elimination for Projected Model Counting\" by Jean-Marie Lagniez, Pierre Marquis, and Armin Biere.\n\nAnother accepted paper was \"Clausal Congruence Closure\" by Armin Biere, Katalin Fazekas, Mathias Fleury, and Nils Froleyks.\n\nFor a complete list of all accepted papers at SAT 2024, you can access the proceedings published in the Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics (LIPIcs).\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"054b5626-cfec-4c93-8627-53621873d58e","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:43:09.647Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"45f1dbd5-0936-45ec-a8f9-1d976ec9ece4","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"1fce334c-f14a-4e8e-90dd-680fb3dfb644","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"49832040-465b-48c3-8853-fc9aaaff2d1f","agentId":"a4438d81d0b74ba38","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Vd8hCQbbFP4BHPdKJUPUtV","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"foundation model\" combinatorial optimization 2025 LLM solver hint\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - ai4co/awesome-fm4co: Recent research papers about Foundation Models for Combinatorial Optimization · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/ai4co/awesome-fm4co\"},{\"title\":\"[2509.16865] Large Language Models as End-to-end Combinatorial Optimization Solvers\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16865\"},{\"title\":\"Large Language Models as End-to-end Combinatorial Optimization Solvers | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/forum?id=qr5uMEs6iR\"},{\"title\":\"[2506.15196] HeurAgenix: Leveraging LLMs for Solving Complex Combinatorial Optimization Challenges\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.15196\"},{\"title\":\"LLM4Solver: Large Language Model for Efficient Algorithm Design of Combinatorial Optimization Solver | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/forum?id=XTxdDEFR6D\"},{\"title\":\"Graph Foundation Models: Bridging Language Model Paradigms and Graph Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2509.24256v1\"},{\"title\":\"LLMOPT: Democratizing Optimization Through Large Language Models | Amir Rajabi\",\"url\":\"https://ahrajabi.github.io/blog/2025/llmopt_optimisation_llms/\"},{\"title\":\"Discovering Heuristics with Large Language Models (LLMs ...\",\"url\":\"https://optimization-online.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Heuristics_Discovery_Via_LLMs_CetinkayaBuyuktahtakinetal2025_OptOnline.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Researchers teach LLMs to solve complex planning challenges | MIT News | Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"url\":\"https://news.mit.edu/2025/researchers-teach-llms-to-solve-complex-planning-challenges-0402\"},{\"title\":\"Large Language Models as End-to-end Combinatorial Optimization Solvers\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2509.16865v1\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here's what I found about foundation models for combinatorial optimization in 2025:\n\n## Key Research Areas\n\nFM4CO research includes using existing Large Language Models for Combinatorial Optimization and building Domain Foundation Models for Combinatorial Optimization.\n\n## LLM-Based Solver Approaches\n\nThere are several notable developments in using LLMs as solvers for combinatorial optimization:\n\n1. **End-to-End CO Solvers**: A novel framework empowers LLMs to serve as end-to-end CO solvers by directly mapping natural language problem descriptions to solutions, using a two-stage training strategy with supervised fine-tuning and feasibility-and-optimality-aware reinforcement learning.\n\n2. **HeurAgenix Framework**: HeurAgenix is a two-stage hyper-heuristic framework powered by large language models that first evolves heuristics and then selects among them automatically.\n\n3. **LLM4Solver**: LLM4Solver is a machine learning framework designed to efficiently design high-quality algorithms of CO solvers by utilizing prior knowledge learned from large language models to search in the space of programming languages.\n\n## Democratization of Optimization\n\nResearchers developed approaches where LLMs could allow nonexperts to use solving algorithms by teaching them to formalize problems, with users only needing to describe the problem in natural language.\n\n## General Research Direction\n\nDeveloping a domain foundation model capable of solving a wide range of combinatorial optimization problems presents a formidable challenge, with recent efforts aiming to create a unified architecture or representation applicable across various COPs.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"8f4466cc-2428-490f-88b8-ada683a13ca3","timestam