{"parentUuid":"50ec9c15-f527-4418-bdb7-b37f00d72d05","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6b51b1f8-77f0-4694-9f17-7a38a67bdbcd","agentId":"a4438d81d0b74ba38","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014obpbuxT4gucpJa3V7eQxX","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"differentiable optimization\" combinatorial 2025 layer neural CP solver\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - Thinklab-SJTU/awesome-ml4co: Awesome machine learning for combinatorial optimization papers. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Thinklab-SJTU/awesome-ml4co\"},{\"title\":\"NeurIPS Poster Geometric Algorithms for Neural Combinatorial Optimization with Constraints\",\"url\":\"https://neurips.cc/virtual/2025/poster/119136\"},{\"title\":\"DIMES: A Differentiable Meta Solver for Combinatorial Optimization Problems | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/forum?id=9u05zr0nhx\"},{\"title\":\"DIMES: A Differentiable Meta Solver for Combinatorial Optimization Problems\",\"url\":\"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2022/file/a3a7387e49f4de290c23beea2dfcdc75-Paper-Conference.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"[2601.06036] Tree-Preconditioned Differentiable Optimization and Axioms as Layers\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.06036\"},{\"title\":\"DiffCoALG@NeurIPS 2025\",\"url\":\"https://sites.google.com/view/diffcoalg2025\"},{\"title\":\"Geometric Algorithms for Neural Combinatorial Optimization with Constraints\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.24039\"},{\"title\":\"[2510.24039] Geometric Algorithms for Neural Combinatorial Optimization with Constraints\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.24039\"},{\"title\":\"Neural combinatorial optimization: A tutorial - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0305054825001303\"},{\"title\":\"Learning to Solve Combinatorial Optimization under Positive Linear Constraints via Non-Autoregressive Neural Networks\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2409.04495\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here are the key findings related to differentiable optimization and combinatorial problems with neural networks and solvers in 2025:\n\n## Recent 2025 Developments\n\nA novel approach called \"Tree-Preconditioned Differentiable Optimization\" involves a Tree-Preconditioned Conjugate Gradient solver that exploits the spanning tree of constraint graphs to whiten ill-conditioned Hessian spectra, achieving superlinear convergence, and formulates the projection as a differentiable layer via the Implicit Function Theorem where the exact Jacobian propagates geometric constraints during backpropagation.\n\nSelf-Supervised Learning for Combinatorial Optimization involves designing an end-to-end differentiable framework that enables solving discrete constrained optimization problems with neural networks by leveraging algorithmic techniques from convex geometry and Carathéodory's theorem to decompose neural network outputs into convex combinations of polytope corners that correspond to feasible sets.\n\n## Related Work in Combinatorial Optimization\n\nTechniques like differentiable constraint encoding, Gumbel reparameterization, and unsupervised learning are being applied in non-autoregressive neural solvers, including building blocks such as graph modeling, GNNs, latent code, Gumbel reparameterization, LinSAT layers, and differentiable objective function estimation.\n\nRecent research has focused on leveraging neural networks to design novel combinatorial algorithms and techniques that allow seamless integration of classic combinatorial algorithms in differentiable neural network architectures, a development commonly referred to as neural combinatorial optimization.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"71c316a6-e9bc-40d9-9a18-3dd96400c06f","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:43:35.097Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"50ec9c15-f527-4418-bdb7-b37f00d72d05","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"7d4a3ca5-046d-40e4-b273-c3abea9def8b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6b51b1f8-77f0-4694-9f17-7a38a67bdbcd","agentId":"a4438d81d0b74ba38","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01F92aMTwaNMRFmizG18Ri4M","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CP-SAT large neighborhood search violation local search 2024 Davies\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"ViolationLS: Constraint-Based Local Search in CP-SAT | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/380927615_ViolationLS_Constraint-Based_Local_Search_in_CP-SAT\"},{\"title\":\"ViolationLS: Constraint-Based Local Search in CP-SAT | Integration of Constraint Programming, Artificial Intelligence, and Operations Research\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1007/978-3-031-60597-0_16\"},{\"title\":\"Constraint-Based Local Search | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/320953385_Constraint-Based_Local_Search\"},{\"title\":\"Laurent PERRON | Tech Lead Operations Research | PhD Computer Science | Google Inc., Mountain View | Google | Research Department | Research profile\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Laurent-Perron-2\"},{\"title\":\"Large Neighborhood Search - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/09_lns.html\"},{\"title\":\"OR-Tools Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://egon.cheme.cmu.edu/ewo/docs/CP-SAT%20and%20OR-Tools.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"ISMP 2024\",\"url\":\"https://ismp2024.gerad.ca/schedule/FB/165\"},{\"title\":\"7.7. Large neighborhood search (LNS): the Job-Shop Problem — or-tools User's Manual\",\"url\":\"https://acrogenesis.com/or-tools/documentation/user_manual/manual/metaheuristics/jobshop_lns.html\"},{\"title\":\"An Investigation of Generic Approaches to Large Neighbourhood Search\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol307-cp2024/LIPIcs.CP.2024.39/LIPIcs.CP.2024.39.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Toby Davies - Google | LinkedIn\",\"url\":\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/tobyodavies/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about your query:\n\nToby O. Davies published \"ViolationLS: Constraint-Based Local Search in CP-SAT\" at the 21st International Conference on Integration of Constraint Programming, Artificial Intelligence, and Operations Research (CPAIOR 2024) in Uppsala, Sweden, May 28–31, 2024.\n\nViolationLS is a Constraint-Based Local Search (CBLS) solver for arbitrary MiniZinc models based on the Feasibility Jump Mixed-Integer Programming Heuristic, and uses a novel approach for finding multi-variable moves without requiring any \"implicit\" or \"one-way\" constraints traditionally used by CBLS solvers.\n\nIt significantly outperforms other state-of-the-art CBLS solvers on the MiniZinc challenge benchmarks, both with and without multi-variable moves.\n\nThe work relates to constraint-based local search techniques integrated into CP-SAT, which is Google's constraint programming solver. While the search results contain references to large neighborhood search methodology and local search approaches in CP-SAT, the specific details about \"violation local search\" and how it extends or differs from traditional approaches can be found in the full research paper published at CPAIOR 2024.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"cd035b47-836c-4cd8-b01c-edf887fb1d92","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:43:35.809Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7d4a3ca5-046d-40e4-b273-c3abea9def8b","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"4e4424ed-50b3-4473-b022-6e8105372c4b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6b51b1f8-77f0-4694-9f17-7a38a67bdbcd","agentId":"a4438d81d0b74ba38","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GVnubZxHLSWzHF3yFk6SyQ","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CP 2025 LIPIcs volume 340 dynamic programming dominance pruning\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"31st International Conference on Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2025)\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/volume/LIPIcs-volume-340\"},{\"title\":\"Transition Dominance in Domain-Independent Dynamic Programming\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.5\"},{\"title\":\"31st International Conference on Principles and Practice of ...\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol340-cp2025/LIPIcs.CP.2025.0/LIPIcs.CP.2025.0.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"2025 Quarterly Report 3 | Association for Constraint Programming\",\"url\":\"https://www.a4cp.org/-97\"},{\"title\":\"Parallel MIP Solving with Dynamic Task Decomposition Peng Lin # Ñ\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol340-cp2025/LIPIcs.CP.2025.26/LIPIcs.CP.2025.26.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"DynamicSAT: Dynamic Configuration Tuning for SAT Solving\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.34\"},{\"title\":\"Anytime and Exact Search for Planning Problems: How to explore a DP-based state transition graph with A*, CP and LS? (Invited Talk)\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.2\"},{\"title\":\"RPID: Rust Programmable Interface for Domain-Independent Dynamic Programming\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.23\"},{\"title\":\"Privacy-Preserving SAT Solving (Invited Talk)\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.1\"},{\"title\":\"52nd International Colloquium on Automata, Languages, and ...\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/volume/LIPIcs-volume-334\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found relevant information about CP 2025 LIPIcs Volume 340:\n\n## Key Result\n\nThe paper \"Transition Dominance in Domain-Independent Dynamic Programming\" by J. Christopher Beck, Ryo Kuroiwa, Jimmy H. M. Lee, Peter J. Stuckey, and Allen Z. Zhong was published in the 31st International Conference on Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2025), which corresponds to LIPIcs Volume 340.\n\n## About the Paper\n\nDomain-independent dynamic programming (DIDP) is a model-based paradigm for dynamic programming (DP) that enables users to define DP models based on a state transition system. The paper formally defines transition dominance in DIDP, where one transition consistently leads to better solutions than another, allowing the search process to safely ignore dominated transitions.\n\n## Recognition\n\nThis paper received the Best Technical Paper Award at CP 2025.\n\nThe full paper is available at the official Dagstuhl repository, with page numbers 5:1-5:23 in the volume.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"6429a3b4-5f17-4bf8-ad5c-7149e1cca7bc","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:43:35.957Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4e4424ed-50b3-4473-b022-6e8105372c4b","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"81e9a27a-79f2-4cd5-aa56-4034ca9791a4","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6b51b1f8-77f0-4694-9f17-7a38a67bdbcd","agentId":"a4438d81d0b74ba38","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JpHw5DELrtMtqh9J1hn88o","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"learning augmented algorithm\" 2025 prediction online optimization warm start\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Competitive strategies to use “warm start” algorithms with ...\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2405.03661\"},{\"title\":\"Competitive strategies to use “warm start” algorithms with predictions\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.03661v1\"},{\"title\":\"Prediction-Specific Design of Learning-Augmented Algorithms\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.14887v1\"},{\"title\":\"An Accelerated Maximum Flow Algorithm with Prediction Enhancement in Dynamic LEO Networks\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/1424-8220/25/8/2555\"},{\"title\":\"[2405.03661] Competitive strategies to use \\\"warm start\\\" algorithms with predictions\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.03661\"},{\"title\":\"Learning-Based Warm-Start Strategies\",\"url\":\"https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/learning-based-warm-start\"},{\"title\":\"[2309.07835] Learning to Warm-Start Fixed-Point Optimization Algorithms\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.07835\"},{\"title\":\"Learning augmented algorithm - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Learning_augmented_algorithm\"},{\"title\":\"Learning to Warm-Start Fixed-Point Optimization Algorithms\",\"url\":\"https://jmlr.org/papers/volume25/23-1174/23-1174.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Competitive strategies to use warm start algorithms with predictions | AI Research Paper Details\",\"url\":\"https://www.aimodels.fyi/papers/arxiv/competitive-strategies-to-use-warm-start-algorithms\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here are the key findings about learning-augmented algorithms, prediction-based optimization, and warm-start strategies:\n\n## Overview of Learning-Augmented Algorithms\n\nAlgorithms with predictions (also learning-augmented algorithms) is a beyond worst-case paradigm of algorithm design that has become well-studied in recent years. A learning augmented algorithm is an algorithm that can make use of a prediction to improve its performance.\n\n## Key Performance Metrics\n\nThe goals are to provide consistency (better performance when predictions are accurate), robustness (performance no worse than worst-case algorithms when predictions are inaccurate), and graceful degradation (smooth performance degradation as prediction quality decreases).\n\n## Warm-Start Algorithms\n\nWarm start algorithms are a practically motivated paradigm that make use of predictions to improve runtime and represent the most common framework in which algorithms with predictions are studied for static problems. An algorithm is given an instance of a problem and a prediction of the solution, with the runtime bounded by the distance from the predicted solution to the true solution.\n\n## Recent Developments (2024-2025)\n\nRecent research includes a learning-augmented warm-start strategy to enhance the Ford–Fulkerson algorithm for dynamic LEO satellite networks, and prediction-specific, strongly-optimal algorithms can significantly improve performance across a variety of online decision-making settings.\n\n## Learning-Based Warm-Start Mechanisms\n\nLearning-based warm-start is a strategy that uses historical data and expert insights to generate an informed initialization for complex algorithms. Machine-learning frameworks can warm-start fixed-point optimization algorithms, consisting of a neural network mapping problem parameters to warm starts, followed by a predefined number of fixed-point iterations.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"94ce5bd6-cf40-4b74-9355-926f42cf80cd","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:43:36.498Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"81e9a27a-79f2-4cd5-aa56-4034ca9791a4","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
