{"parentUuid":"3a8a97c0-a3d9-4ff7-a0cc-7bc8d0fbee7f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6b51b1f8-77f0-4694-9f17-7a38a67bdbcd","agentId":"a4438d81d0b74ba38","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QDoo3GZRnhsQYVK2vpau2E","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"DynamicSAT\" dynamic configuration tuning SAT 2025 Lin paper\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"DynamicSAT: Dynamic Configuration Tuning for SAT Solving\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.34\"},{\"title\":\"DynamicSAT: Dynamic Configuration Tuning for SAT Solving Zhengyuan Shi #\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol340-cp2025/LIPIcs.CP.2025.34/LIPIcs.CP.2025.34.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - cure-lab/DynamicSAT\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/cure-lab/DynamicSAT\"},{\"title\":\"Xindi Zhang(张昕荻)'s Homepage - Homepage\",\"url\":\"https://dezhangxd.github.io/\"},{\"title\":\"SAT: Dynamic Spatial Aptitude Training for Multimodal Language Models\",\"url\":\"https://arijitray.com/SAT/\"},{\"title\":\"SAT-GATv2: A Dynamic Attention-Based Graph Neural Network for Solving Boolean Satisfiability Problem\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2079-9292/14/3/423\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - jwwthu/Satellite-Open-Source: This is the repository for the collection of open source code and data for satellite communication. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/jwwthu/Satellite-Open-Source\"},{\"title\":\"Automated Algorithm Configuration and Parameter Tuning | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-642-21434-9_3\"},{\"title\":\"[2412.07755] SAT: Dynamic Spatial Aptitude Training for Multimodal Language Models\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.07755\"},{\"title\":\"DYNAMIC MIXTURE OF EXPERTS: AN AUTO-TUNING ...\",\"url\":\"https://proceedings.iclr.cc/paper_files/paper/2025/file/c63ff34c163bca3055d39490f6d1aaf7-Paper-Conference.pdf\"}]\n\nI found relevant information about the DynamicSAT paper. Here are the search results:\n\n## DynamicSAT: Dynamic Configuration Tuning for SAT Solving\n\nThe paper \"DynamicSAT: Dynamic Configuration Tuning for SAT Solving\" was authored by Zhengyuan Shi, Wentao Jiang, Xindi Zhang, Jin Luo, Yun Liang, Zhufei Chu, and Qiang Xu, and was presented at the 31st International Conference on Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2025).\n\n### Key Contribution\n\nDynamicSAT is a novel SAT solver framework that dynamically tunes configuration parameters during the solving process, adapting to changes arising from clause learning, elimination, and other transformations.\n\n### Problem Address\n\nA major limitation of previous approaches is that a configuration that performs well on one instance may lead to inefficiencies on others, and while previous approaches to automatic algorithm configuration set parameters prior to runtime, they fail to adapt to the dynamic evolution of problem characteristics during the solving process.\n\n### Technical Approach\n\nThe approach ensures a balance between exploration and exploitation while maintaining computational efficiency and flexibility, defining hyperparameters θ (formula change threshold), d (intervals of MAB decision making), and μ (sampling frequency).\n\nThe official code repository is available on GitHub at [cure-lab/DynamicSAT](https://github.com/cure-lab/DynamicSAT).\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"36080f1d-ca21-4c23-9ae4-af2f5e6edb1e","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:43:46.207Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3a8a97c0-a3d9-4ff7-a0cc-7bc8d0fbee7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"245ed42a-c9ad-4429-ba62-463d1fa2bfb1","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6b51b1f8-77f0-4694-9f17-7a38a67bdbcd","agentId":"a4438d81d0b74ba38","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GzhZrhQm61iS1bVbvCman5","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"SAT 2025 LIPIcs volume papers list Streamlining Distributed\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Streamlining Distributed SAT Solver Design - DROPS\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.SAT.2025.27\"},{\"title\":\"Proceedings & Accepted Papers - SAT 2025\",\"url\":\"https://satisfiability.org/SAT25/papers/\"},{\"title\":\"Streamlining Distributed SAT Solver Design Dominik Schreiber # Ñ\",\"url\":\"https://cca.informatik.uni-freiburg.de/papers/SchreiberRigiLupertiBiere-SAT25.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"28th International Conference on Theory and Applications of Satisfiability Testing (SAT 2025)\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/volume/LIPIcs-volume-341\"},{\"title\":\"Calls - SAT 2025\",\"url\":\"http://satisfiability.org/SAT25/cfp/\"},{\"title\":\"SAT 2025 Call for Papers\",\"url\":\"http://www.satlive.org/2025/03/07/sat25-cfp.html\"},{\"title\":\"28th International Conference on Theory and Applications of ...\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol341-sat2025/LIPIcs.SAT.2025/LIPIcs.SAT.2025.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"39th International Symposium on Distributed Computing (DISC 2025)\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/volume/LIPIcs-volume-356\"},{\"title\":\"Privacy-Preserving SAT Solving (Invited Talk)\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.1\"},{\"title\":\"Streamlining Distributed SAT Solver Design\",\"url\":\"https://satres.kikit.kit.edu/slides/2025-sat-streamlining.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about the paper \"Streamlining Distributed SAT Solver Design\" in the SAT 2025 LIPIcs volume.\n\n## Paper Details\n\nThe paper \"Streamlining Distributed SAT Solver Design\" was authored by Dominik Schreiber, Niccolò Rigi-Luperti, and Armin Biere, and appears in the 28th International Conference on Theory and Applications of Satisfiability Testing (SAT 2025), published in volume 341 of Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics (LIPIcs), pages 27:1–27:23.\n\n## Paper Overview\n\nThe paper addresses distributed clause-sharing SAT solvers, examining how a common design runs many off-the-shelf sequential solvers in parallel with diversification and conflict clause sharing, noting that this approach naïvely adopts sequential solver practices that may be less useful or even detrimental in distributed settings.\n\n## SAT 2025 Papers List\n\nFor a comprehensive list of all papers in the SAT 2025 LIPIcs volume, the proceedings are published as volume 341 of LIPIcs and available at: https://www.dagstuhl.de/dagpub/978-3-95977-381-2.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"80207d1f-3a2c-4a45-804e-799657688fa1","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:43:46.621Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"245ed42a-c9ad-4429-ba62-463d1fa2bfb1","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"ab12b09d-7b97-406f-9350-baac9f97f1fd","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6b51b1f8-77f0-4694-9f17-7a38a67bdbcd","agentId":"a4438d81d0b74ba38","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013XxvZQ2AnjzccEiSkWnQDy","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"Dependency-Curated Large Neighbourhood Search\" Lewander CP 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Dependency-Curated Large Neighbourhood Search\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.20\"},{\"title\":\"Dependency-Curated Large Neighbourhood Search Frej Knutar Lewander1 #\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol340-cp2025/LIPIcs.CP.2025.20/LIPIcs.CP.2025.20.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Large Neighborhood Search - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/09_lns.html\"},{\"title\":\"An Investigation of Generic Approaches to Large Neighbourhood Search\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol307-cp2024/LIPIcs.CP.2024.39/LIPIcs.CP.2024.39.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Large Neighborhood Search\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/226905030_Large_Neighborhood_Search\"},{\"title\":\"Adaptive Large Neighborhood Search\",\"url\":\"https://d-nb.info/1072464683/34\"},{\"title\":\"Adaptive large neighborhood search for mixed integer programming | Mathematical Programming Computation | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12532-021-00209-7\"},{\"title\":\"A Stochastic Sequence-Dependent Disassembly Line Balancing Problem with an Adaptive Large Neighbourhood Search Algorithm\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2227-9717/13/6/1675\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - N-Wouda/ALNS: Adaptive large neighbourhood search (and more!) in Python. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/N-Wouda/ALNS\"},{\"title\":\"Large Neighborhood Search | Choco-solver\",\"url\":\"https://choco-solver.org/docs/solving/lns/\"}]\n\nI found the paper for you! Here are the key details about \"Dependency-Curated Large Neighbourhood Search\":\n\n## Publication Information\n\nThe paper was authored by Frej Knutar Lewander, Pierre Flener, and Justin Pearson and was published in the 31st International Conference on Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2025), in Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics (LIPIcs), Volume 340, pp. 20:1-20:17.\n\n## Overview\n\nIn large neighbourhood search (LNS), an incumbent initial solution is incrementally improved by selecting a subset of the variables, called the freeze set, and fixing them to their values in the incumbent solution, while a value for each remaining variable is found and assigned via solving (such as constraint programming-style propagation and search).\n\n## Main Contribution\n\nThe paper presents a curation scheme which exploits variable dependencies to find a low-cardinality set of variables that the freeze set of any selection heuristic should be a subset of, and the scheme often improves the overall performance of generic selection heuristics. The experiments show that the performance of using the dependency curation scheme with the naïve randomised selection heuristic is competitive with the more elaborate state-of-the-art generic selection heuristics, even when they use the scheme.\n\nThe paper is available at the [Dagstuhl repository](https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.20).\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"59f3c263-0d6d-4544-8534-94af66dd1e16","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:43:46.974Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ab12b09d-7b97-406f-9350-baac9f97f1fd","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"67dc4945-58dd-4d9a-b4fb-7af044c0c986","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6b51b1f8-77f0-4694-9f17-7a38a67bdbcd","agentId":"a4438d81d0b74ba38","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Lxq4F6Fmyp5DoYsuex3Nit","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"FunSearch\" OR \"EvolutionaryCO\" LLM heuristic discovery placement 2024 DeepMind\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"FunSearch: Making new discoveries in mathematical sciences using Large Language Models — Google DeepMind\",\"url\":\"https://deepmind.google/blog/funsearch-making-new-discoveries-in-mathematical-sciences-using-large-language-models/\"},{\"title\":\"Mathematical discoveries from program search with large language models | Nature\",\"url\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-06924-6\"},{\"title\":\"This is the author’s version of the work accepted in Nature.\",\"url\":\"https://storage.googleapis.com/deepmind-media/DeepMind.com/Blog/funsearch-making-new-discoveries-in-mathematical-sciences-using-large-language-models/Mathematical-discoveries-from-program-search-with-large-language-models.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"FunSearch — How Deepmind uses LLM to solve a Mathematic problem that has never been solved | by Fisher Lok | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://fisherlok.medium.com/funsearch-how-deepmind-uses-llm-to-solve-a-mathematic-problem-that-has-never-been-solved-17a9f705f771\"},{\"title\":\"FunSearch: Making new discoveries in mathematical sciences using Large Language Models - Google DeepMind\",\"url\":\"https://deepmind.google/discover/blog/funsearch-making-new-discoveries-in-mathematical-sciences-using-large-language-models/\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Mathematical discoveries from program search with large language models\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/376546904_Mathematical_discoveries_from_program_search_with_large_language_models\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - google-deepmind/funsearch · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google-deepmind/funsearch\"},{\"title\":\"FunSearch.pdf - Computer Science\",\"url\":\"https://cs.nyu.edu/~davise/papers/FunSearch.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Mathematical discoveries from program search with large language models - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10794145/\"},{\"title\":\"Can We Use AI to Discover Better Algorithms? | Richard Cornelius Suwandi\",\"url\":\"https://richardcsuwandi.github.io/blog/2025/llm-algorithm-discovery/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about FunSearch and related research from DeepMind:\n\n## FunSearch Overview\n\nFunSearch is a method to search for new solutions in mathematics and computer science. FunSearch is an evolutionary procedure based on pairing a pretrained LLM with a systematic evaluator.\n\n## Key Applications\n\nThe research demonstrated FunSearch's effectiveness in two main areas:\n\n1. **Extremal Combinatorics**: Applying FunSearch to the cap set problem, they discovered new constructions of large cap sets going beyond the best-known ones, both in finite dimensional and asymptotic cases.\n\n2. **Bin Packing**: FunSearch was used to discover a program that corresponds to a more efficient heuristic for online bin packing. FunSearch delivered an automatically tailored program that outperformed established heuristics – using fewer bins to pack the same number of items.\n\n## Publication and Updates\n\nIn December 2024, DeepMind published a report on arXiv showing how their method can be used to amplify human performance in combinatorial competitive programming.\n\n## Technical Approach\n\nFunSearch integrates Large Language Models (LLMs) with evolutionary algorithms to mimic the progressive nature of scientific research. The system maintains a program database and uses evolutionary techniques to improve discovered solutions iteratively.\n\nNote: The search did not return results specifically about \"EvolutionaryCO,\" so the results focus on FunSearch, which appears to be the primary DeepMind work in this area for 2024.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"c35f5a04-541a-431d-9e5f-8b7b359060b4","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:43:49.114Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"67dc4945-58dd-4d9a-b4fb-7af044c0c986","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
