{"parentUuid":"6cb4a496-bafe-4580-9f90-13c222f18a48","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6b51b1f8-77f0-4694-9f17-7a38a67bdbcd","agentId":"a4438d81d0b74ba38","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SFNi6mfCqyNUCAaLNBAHm7","type":"tool_result","content":"# SAT 2025 Papers: Focused Research Areas\n\nBased on the provided proceedings content, I found limited papers explicitly addressing your specified topics. Here are the relevant papers identified:\n\n## Papers with Matching Focus Areas\n\n**1. Learn to Unlearn**\n- First Author: Bernhard Gstrein\n- DOI: 10.4230/LIPIcs.SAT.2025.14\n- Focus: Learned clause management and recycling strategies\n\n**2. Depth-Optimal Quantum Layout Synthesis as SAT**\n- First Author: Anna B. Jakobsen\n- DOI: 10.4230/LIPIcs.SAT.2025.16\n- Focus: \"Incremental SAT solving and parallel plans for an efficient encoding\"\n\n**3. Problem Partitioning via Proof Prefixes**\n- First Author: Zachary Battleman\n- DOI: 10.4230/LIPIcs.SAT.2025.3\n- Focus: Massively parallel SAT solving through automated problem partitioning\n\n## Note\n\nThe provided content appears to be truncated (indicated by \"[Content truncated due to length...]\"), so additional papers addressing GPU acceleration, IPASIR-UP standards, propagators, preprocessing techniques, symmetry breaking, or machine learning approaches may exist in the complete proceedings but are not visible in this excerpt."}]},"uuid":"f03b848c-e3d6-4b60-9da1-a5403cc9faf6","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:44:00.998Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6cb4a496-bafe-4580-9f90-13c222f18a48","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"6502f61c-1bba-4b71-865d-9b9b16130a90","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6b51b1f8-77f0-4694-9f17-7a38a67bdbcd","agentId":"a4438d81d0b74ba38","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DuozpVDKTQT5XK8objp6nx","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"Conflict Analysis Cutting Planes\" CP 2025 Baauw Flippo Demirovic\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Conflict Analysis Based on Cutting-Planes for Constraint Programming\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.4\"},{\"title\":\"31st International Conference on Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2025)\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/volume/LIPIcs-volume-340\"},{\"title\":\"CP 2025 Accepted Papers\",\"url\":\"https://cp2025.a4cp.org/accepted_papers.html\"},{\"title\":\"Conflict Analysis Based on Cutting Planes for Constraint ...\",\"url\":\"https://repository.tudelft.nl/file/File_6cb76175-7821-435a-b340-e1621a8450ef\"},{\"title\":\"Improving Reduction Techniques in Pseudo-Boolean Conflict Analysis\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.SAT.2025.21\"},{\"title\":\"Cut-Based Conflict Analysis in Mixed Integer Programming | INFORMS Journal on Computing\",\"url\":\"https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/ijoc.2024.0999\"},{\"title\":\"Improving Conflict Analysis in MIP Solvers by Pseudo-Boolean Reasoning\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2023.27\"},{\"title\":\"Towards Modern and Modular SAT for LCG (Short Paper)\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.42\"},{\"title\":\"[2410.15110] Cut-based Conflict Analysis in Mixed Integer Programming\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.15110\"},{\"title\":\"[PDF] Cutting to the Core of Pseudo-Boolean Optimization: Combining Core-Guided Search with Cutting Planes Reasoning | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Cutting-to-the-Core-of-Pseudo-Boolean-Optimization:-Devriendt-Gocht/02eb66325ae688080a5d6ef491ba9cf6f8def4fc\"}]\n\nI found the research paper you're looking for! Here are the results:\n\n## Paper Information\n\nThe paper \"Conflict Analysis Based on Cutting-Planes for Constraint Programming\" was authored by Robbin Baauw, Maarten Flippo, and Emir Demirović and was presented at the 31st International Conference on Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2025).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nThis paper introduces a novel constraint learning mechanism for Constraint Programming (CP) solvers that integrates cutting planes reasoning into the conflict analysis procedure. Their approach, named Lazy Linear Generation (LLG), can generate linear integer inequalities to prune the search space, rather than propositional clauses as in Lazy Clause Generation (LCG). \n\nThis combines the strengths of constraint programming (strong propagation through global constraints) with cutting-planes reasoning.\n\n## Publication Details\n\nThe paper is part of the Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics (LIPIcs) series, Volume 340. The paper is available at: https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.4\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"f3197f29-4bcf-4bab-8bd9-1f97b631ac24","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:44:01.764Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6502f61c-1bba-4b71-865d-9b9b16130a90","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"e2144b80-bf4b-4307-87d9-23b2e460a960","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6b51b1f8-77f0-4694-9f17-7a38a67bdbcd","agentId":"a4438d81d0b74ba38","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SrUhMnBMt75rJwvZsY31uc","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"SoCG 2025 packing computational geometry rectangle empty space algorithm\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Symposium on Computational Geometry (SoCG 2025)\",\"url\":\"https://socg25.github.io/socg.html\"},{\"title\":\"SoCG: Symposium on Computational Geometry 2027 2026 2025 ...\",\"url\":\"http://www.wikicfp.com/cfp/program?id=2722\"},{\"title\":\"SoCG 2025\",\"url\":\"https://socg25.hotcrp.com/\"},{\"title\":\"Computational Geometry\",\"url\":\"https://computational-geometry.org/cg-week/challenge/\"},{\"title\":\"CG:SHOP SoCG Competition\",\"url\":\"https://cgshop.ibr.cs.tu-bs.de/\"},{\"title\":\"SoCG 2025 : Symposium on Computational Geometry\",\"url\":\"http://www.wikicfp.com/cfp/servlet/event.showcfp?copyownerid=90704&eventid=182527\"},{\"title\":\"SoCG 2026 - CG Week 2026 - Computational Geometry\",\"url\":\"https://cgweek26.computational-geometry.org/contribute/\"},{\"title\":\"Computational Geometry\",\"url\":\"https://www.computational-geometry.org/\"},{\"title\":\"Anchored Rectangle and Square Packings\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.SoCG.2016.13\"},{\"title\":\"SoCG 2025: ACM Symposium on Computational Geometry\",\"url\":\"https://www.myhuiban.com/conference/382?lang=en_us\"}]\n\nBased on my search results, here's what I found about SoCG 2025:\n\nThe 41st International Symposium on Computational Geometry (SoCG 2025) is planned to be held in Kanazawa, Japan, June 23–27, 2025, as part of the Computational Geometry (CG) Week.\n\nRegarding the specific topic of rectangle packing and empty space algorithms you mentioned, the search results show a related paper from an earlier SoCG conference. In a 2016 SoCG paper, researchers explored anchored rectangle packing, which consists of interior-disjoint axis-aligned empty rectangles in the unit square such that each point is a corner of a rectangle.\n\nThe conference accepts submissions across different types of contributions. Papers containing algorithms, data structures, theorems, proofs, or lower bound constructions on computational geometry problems are evaluated on the relevance and importance of the problem studied, technical depth, elegance of the solution, and potential impact of the results.\n\nHowever, I did not find specific information about papers being presented at SoCG 2025 on packing algorithms with rectangles and empty space. If you're looking for accepted papers for the 2025 conference, you may want to check the official SoCG 2025 proceedings once they are published.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"7d7c6f0c-6da5-4710-aa53-f6dfe2d96e68","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:44:02.898Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e2144b80-bf4b-4307-87d9-23b2e460a960","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"0bfa6637-8690-4a5a-8ac8-479466791e14","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6b51b1f8-77f0-4694-9f17-7a38a67bdbcd","agentId":"a4438d81d0b74ba38","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DEuxsZiYePkCQMKVb27qtb","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"Transformer-based Feature Learning Algorithm Selection\" Pellegrino CP 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Transformer-Based Feature Learning for Algorithm Selection in Combinatorial Optimisation\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.31\"},{\"title\":\"Transformer-Based Feature Learning for Algorithm Selection ...\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol340-cp2025/LIPIcs.CP.2025.31/LIPIcs.CP.2025.31.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"CP 2025 Accepted Papers\",\"url\":\"https://cp2025.a4cp.org/accepted_papers.html\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - Thinklab-SJTU/awesome-ml4co: Awesome machine learning for combinatorial optimization papers. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Thinklab-SJTU/awesome-ml4co\"},{\"title\":\"Two-Stage Feature Generation with Transformer and Reinforcement Learning | IJCAI\",\"url\":\"https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2025/577\"},{\"title\":\"Benchmarking Transformer-Based and Conventional Machine Learning Models for Cardiovascular Disease Prediction on Datasets of Varying Scale and Complexity | medRxiv\",\"url\":\"https://www.medrxiv.org/content/10.1101/2025.08.03.25332878v1.full\"},{\"title\":\"Attentive transformer deep learning algorithm for intrusion detection on IoT systems using automatic Xplainable feature selection | PLOS One\",\"url\":\"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0286652\"},{\"title\":\"Transformers as statisticians: provable in-context learning ...\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.5555/3666122.3668620\"},{\"title\":\"Enhancing Transformer-based models for long sequence time series forecasting via structured matrix - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0925231225001018\"},{\"title\":\"[2306.04637] Transformers as Statisticians: Provable In-Context Learning with In-Context Algorithm Selection\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.04637\"}]\n\nI found the paper you're looking for! Here are the search results:\n\nThe paper \"Transformer-Based Feature Learning for Algorithm Selection in Combinatorial Optimisation\" was authored by Alessio Pellegrino, Özgür Akgün, Nguyen Dang, Zeynep Kiziltan, and Ian Miguel, and was presented at the 31st International Conference on Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2025).\n\n**Key Details:**\n- The paper is published in Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics (LIPIcs), Volume 340, pp. 31:1-31:22\n- The paper addresses the problem that for combinatorial optimization problems there are typically multiple ways to model them for an automated solver, and choosing the right combination of model and solver can significantly impact effectiveness. The authors build machine learning models to automatically select the best combination for a problem instance.\n- The approach explores automatic feature learning for algorithm selection and employs a transformer encoder to learn instance features directly from a high-level representation.\n\nThe full paper is available at: https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.31\n\nThe source code repository is also available at: https://github.com/SeppiaBrilla/EFE_project\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"0989f8ab-5103-49c5-99ca-efef74e3d4d9","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:44:03.952Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0bfa6637-8690-4a5a-8ac8-479466791e14","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
