{"parentUuid":"efc453b9-6e72-48a6-9ccc-572200d5de66","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"b19e4404-614c-4e91-846d-da46b837829e","agentId":"a4438d81d0b74ba38","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Eu2a8eu39cGWMaYGsp9Shy","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"in-context learning\" combinatorial optimization 2025 reasoning chain DeepSeek o1\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"DeepSeek-R1: Incentivizing Reasoning Capability in LLMs via Reinforcement Learning\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2501.12948v1\"},{\"title\":\"DeepSeek-R1 incentivizes reasoning in LLMs through reinforcement learning | Nature\",\"url\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-09422-z\"},{\"title\":\"Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO)\",\"url\":\"https://cameronrwolfe.substack.com/p/grpo\"},{\"title\":\"DeepSeek-R1: Incentivizing Reasoning Capability in LLMs via\",\"url\":\"https://www.thewirechina.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/DeepSeek-R1-Document.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"[2501.12948] DeepSeek-R1: Incentivizing Reasoning Capability in LLMs via Reinforcement Learning\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12948\"},{\"title\":\"An Introduction to Reasoning Models with DeepSeek R1 Anoop Kunchukuttan\",\"url\":\"https://anoopkunchukuttan.gitlab.io/publications/presentations/DeepSeek-OSSProjects-Intro-Feb2025.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"[2402.03300] DeepSeekMath: Pushing the Limits of Mathematical Reasoning in Open Language Models\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.03300\"},{\"title\":\"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-R1 · Hugging Face\",\"url\":\"https://huggingface.co/deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-R1\"},{\"title\":\"Reasoning Beyond Limits: Advances and Open Problems for LLMs\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2503.22732v1\"},{\"title\":\"Yet Another DeepSeek Overview - Tamanna Hossain-Kay\",\"url\":\"https://www.tamanna-hossain-kay.com/post/2025/02/08/deepseek/\"}]\n\nI've successfully performed a web search on your query. Here's a summary of the key findings:\n\n## Search Results Overview\n\nThe search returned recent information about DeepSeek-R1 and reasoning models, with several relevant findings:\n\n### DeepSeek-R1 and Reasoning Chains\n\nOpenAI's o1 series models were the first to introduce inference-time scaling by increasing the length of the Chain-of-Thought reasoning process. DeepSeek-R1-Zero and DeepSeek-R1 are first-generation reasoning models, with DeepSeek-R1-Zero trained via large-scale reinforcement learning (RL) without supervised fine-tuning as a preliminary step.\n\n### Performance and Capabilities\n\nDeepSeek-R1 achieves performance comparable to OpenAI-o1-1217 on reasoning tasks. The reasoning abilities of LLMs can be incentivized through pure reinforcement learning, obviating the need for human-labelled reasoning trajectories, and the RL framework facilitates the emergent development of advanced reasoning patterns, such as self-reflection, verification and dynamic strategy adaptation.\n\n### Reasoning Behaviors\n\nDeepSeek-R1-Zero demonstrates capabilities such as self-verification, reflection, and generating long CoTs, and is the first open research to validate that reasoning capabilities of LLMs can be incentivized purely through RL, without the need for SFT.\n\nThe search results focus primarily on DeepSeek-R1's reasoning capabilities and reinforcement learning approaches. However, the results don't contain specific information about \"in-context learning\" or \"combinatorial optimization\" in relation to these models.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"a959dc3a-da53-4386-b188-b6b065e7c3e2","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:44:14.151Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"efc453b9-6e72-48a6-9ccc-572200d5de66","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"ff5e23d4-297a-49bd-a66b-33bc41bf6c14","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"b19e4404-614c-4e91-846d-da46b837829e","agentId":"a4438d81d0b74ba38","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NpunoEgJGMJPAs44G2jDHZ","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"Parallel MIP Solving\" \"Dynamic Task Decomposition\" Peng Lin CP 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Parallel MIP Solving with Dynamic Task Decomposition\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.26\"},{\"title\":\"Parallel MIP Solving with Dynamic Task Decomposition Peng Lin # Ñ\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol340-cp2025/LIPIcs.CP.2025.26/LIPIcs.CP.2025.26.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - shaowei-cai-group/PartiMIP: Parallel MIP Solving with Dynamic Task Decomposition · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/shaowei-cai-group/PartiMIP\"},{\"title\":\"Peng Lin - Peng Lin\",\"url\":\"https://linpeng0105.github.io/\"},{\"title\":\"31st International Conference on Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2025)\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/volume/LIPIcs-volume-340\"},{\"title\":\"CP 2025 Accepted Papers\",\"url\":\"https://cp2025.a4cp.org/accepted_papers.html\"},{\"title\":\"Optimization of resource-aware parallel and distributed computing: a review | The Journal of Supercomputing | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11227-025-07295-7\"},{\"title\":\"ICML 2025 Papers\",\"url\":\"https://icml.cc/virtual/2025/papers.html\"},{\"title\":\"Parallel Solvers for Mixed Integer Linear Optimization | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-319-63516-3_8\"},{\"title\":\"Research on computing task scheduling method for distributed heterogeneous parallel systems | Scientific Reports\",\"url\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-94068-0\"}]\n\nI found relevant results for your search. Here's what I discovered:\n\n## Overview\n\nThe paper \"Parallel MIP Solving with Dynamic Task Decomposition\" was authored by Peng Lin, Shaowei Cai, Mengchuan Zou, and Shengqi Chen and presented at the 31st International Conference on Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2025).\n\n## Framework Details\n\nThe work proposes a novel parallel MIP solving framework that employs dynamic task decomposition in a divide-and-conquer paradigm. The framework incorporates a hardness estimate heuristic to identify challenging solving tasks and a reward decaying mechanism to reinforce the task decomposition decision.\n\n## Implementation and Results\n\nThe framework was applied to two state-of-the-art open-source MIP solvers, SCIP and HiGHS, yielding efficient parallel solvers. PartiMIP is an innovative framework for parallel mixed integer programming (MIP) solving that achieves efficient parallelization through dynamic task decomposition.\n\nThe experiments were carried out on a server equipped with 2 AMD EPYC 9654 CPUs and 2048 GB of RAM, running Ubuntu 20.04.4, with each solver executed with a 300-second time limit per instance. The solver, evaluation scripts, related experimental results, and new best known solutions have been made available on GitHub.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"3ef06c8e-99ad-4899-86a8-e750c75d2d7b","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:44:15.342Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ff5e23d4-297a-49bd-a66b-33bc41bf6c14","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"a5db6b3c-d1e7-4147-b7d5-f914298fc51f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"b19e4404-614c-4e91-846d-da46b837829e","agentId":"a4438d81d0b74ba38","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EBAgGipaV3bozseZ6djJRg","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"Towards Modern and Modular SAT for LCG\" CP 2025 short paper\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Towards Modern and Modular SAT for LCG (Short Paper)\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.42\"},{\"title\":\"Google Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Towards+Modern+and+Modular+SAT+for+LCG+(Short+Paper).\"},{\"title\":\"Towards Modern and Modular SAT for LCG Jip J. Dekker #\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol340-cp2025/LIPIcs.CP.2025.42/LIPIcs.CP.2025.42.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Towards Modern and Modular SAT for LCG\",\"url\":\"https://alexeyignatiev.github.io/assets/pdf/disz-cp25-preprint.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Towards Modern and Modular SAT for LCG (Short Paper). | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/search?q=Towards+Modern+and+Modular+SAT+for+LCG+(Short+Paper).\"},{\"title\":\"CP 2025 Accepted Papers\",\"url\":\"https://cp2025.a4cp.org/accepted_papers.html\"},{\"title\":\"31st International Conference on Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2025)\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/volume/LIPIcs-volume-340\"},{\"title\":\"CiteSeerX\",\"url\":\"https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/search_result?query=Towards+Modern+and+Modular+SAT+for+LCG+(Short+Paper).\"},{\"title\":\"Calls - SAT 2025\",\"url\":\"http://satisfiability.org/SAT25/cfp/\"},{\"title\":\"SAT 2025 - CFP\",\"url\":\"https://easychair.org/cfp/SAT2025\"}]\n\nI found the paper you're looking for. Here are the details:\n\n**\"Towards Modern and Modular SAT for LCG\"** is a short paper by Jip J. Dekker, Alexey Ignatiev, Peter J. Stuckey, and Allen Z. Zhong, presented at the 31st International Conference on Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2025).\n\nThe paper appears in Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics (LIPIcs), Volume 340, pages 42:1-42:12, published by Schloss Dagstuhl – Leibniz-Zentrum für Informatik in 2025.\n\n**Overview:**\nThe paper addresses Lazy Clause Generation (LCG), an architecture for building Constraint Programming (CP) solvers using an underlying Boolean Satisfiability (SAT) engine. The authors note that SAT infrastructure in LCG solvers has been based on technology from at least a decade ago, while SAT solvers have made significant advances including inprocessing, chronological backtracking, and improvements to search strategies. They investigate how a more modern conflict-driven clause learning (CDCL) SAT solver impacts the design choices of Lazy Clause Generation solvers.\n\nThe paper makes use of the IPASIR-UP interface for extending SAT solvers, exploring the possibilities and challenges of using IPASIR-UP for LCG and showing that it can be used to create a highly competitive solver.\n\nThe paper and associated source code (Huub solver) are available from the Dagstuhl website.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"04c74dcf-5d9d-4b11-a386-048beb0e9ea6","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:44:16.535Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a5db6b3c-d1e7-4147-b7d5-f914298fc51f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"04c74dcf-5d9d-4b11-a386-048beb0e9ea6","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a4438d81d0b74ba38","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01W44u7YS6KCCtCHpAd9gsyM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have plenty of solid material. Let me compose the final report.\n\n---\n\n# 调研结果：2024-2026 学术界 CP/SAT/ML4CO 没覆盖的金矿\n\n下面 12 个 specific 工作，按\"对 70x70 placement 项目可借鉴度\"评分（1-5 分，5 = 立刻能上）。\n\n## 一、CP/CPAIOR 直接相关\n\n**1. Baauw, Flippo, Demirović — \"Conflict Analysis Based on Cutting-Planes for Constraint Programming\" (CP 2025, LIPIcs.CP.2025.4)** ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐\n提出 LLG（Lazy Linear Generation），把 cutting-planes reasoning 接进 CP conflict analysis，学到的不是 clause 而是线性整数不等式，剪枝比 LCG 强。我们的 master-binding-routing-flow 全是整数约束，把 ghost rectangle 那套从 nogood-clause 升级成 cutting-plane learning，理论上能减少 Benders cut 数量。\n\n**2. Dekker, Ignatiev, Stuckey, Zhong — \"Towards Modern and Modular SAT for LCG\" (CP 2025 短论文, LIPIcs.CP.2025.42)** ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐\n他们用 IPASIR-UP 把现代 CDCL SAT（chronological backtracking + inprocessing）接进 LCG，开源 Huub solver，挑战 OR-Tools/Chuffed。我们 Round 1+2 只覆盖了 IPASIR-UP API 本身，没看 LCG 怎么用它做 placement-style 全局约束——这个直接是我们项目同形态。\n\n**3. Lin, Cai, Zou, Chen — \"Parallel MIP Solving with Dynamic Task Decomposition\" (CP 2025, LIPIcs.CP.2025.26, github: shaowei-cai-group/PartiMIP)** ⭐⭐⭐⭐\nPartiMIP 框架，divide-and-conquer + 难度估计 heuristic + reward decay 决定怎么切任务，套到 SCIP/HiGHS 上。我们 S6 在调 stage-specific worker matrix，本质是静态切，可以借 PartiMIP 的动态切策略给 outer-search candidate loop 用。\n\n**4. Lewander, Flener, Pearson — \"Dependency-Curated Large Neighbourhood Search\" (CP 2025, LIPIcs.CP.2025.20)** ⭐⭐⭐⭐\nLNS 的 freeze set 选择问题：他们用变量依赖图找一个低基数 superset，约束所有 freeze set 都必须是它子集。结果表明随机 freeze + 这套 curation = 持平 SOTA selection。我们 placement 变量天然有 spatial dependency 图，直接套。\n\n**5. Davies — \"ViolationLS: Constraint-Based Local Search in CP-SAT\" (CPAIOR 2024, LNCS 14743)** ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐\nGoogle 自家 Toby Davies 把 Feasibility Jump 启发式以 multi-variable move 形式接进 CP-SAT，MiniZinc 挑战榜大胜其他 CBLS。我们直接用 OR-Tools CP-SAT，这个是同源工作，可以查 CP-SAT 9.x 之后的 violation_ls 参数有没有暴露出来。\n\n**6. Tardivo, Michel, Pontelli — \"CP for Bin Packing with Multi-core and GPUs\" (CP 2024, LIPIcs.CP.2024)** ⭐⭐⭐⭐\nCP solver 跑 bin packing 用 GPU 加速 propagation，开源。我们 70x70 grid 本质就是 2D bin packing，他们的 GPU propagator 模式可以参考——尤其考虑我们 candidate ordering 那条主线已经在做大批量 enumeration。\n\n## 二、SAT 直接相关\n\n**7. Shi, Jiang, Zhang et al. — \"DynamicSAT: Dynamic Configuration Tuning for SAT Solving\" (CP 2025, LIPIcs.CP.2025.34, github: cure-lab/DynamicSAT)** ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐\n求解过程中用 multi-armed bandit 动态切 SAT 参数，根据公式 evolution（clause learning/elimination）调。直接是我们 S6 stage-specific worker matrix 的升级版——从\"按 stage 静态分\"升级到\"按运行时 formula 状态在线切\"。\n\n**8. Schreiber, Rigi-Luperti, Biere — \"Streamlining Distributed SAT Solver Design\" (SAT 2025, LIPIcs.SAT.2025.27)** ⭐⭐⭐\n把 LBD-based clause sharing 从 distributed 设置中拿掉，单核 preprocessing 与并行 search 并行跑。我们用 4-process 并行 wave，clause sharing 策略可能是 bottleneck，值得参考。\n\n**9. Battleman et al. — \"Problem Partitioning via Proof Prefixes\" (SAT 2025, LIPIcs.SAT.2025.3)** ⭐⭐⭐\nmassively parallel SAT 自动切问题。比 cube-and-conquer 更精，对我们 outer candidate loop 的 partition 策略有启发。\n\n## 三、ML4CO 新方向\n\n**10. Pellegrino, Akgün, Dang, Kiziltan, Miguel — \"Transformer-Based Feature Learning for Algorithm Selection in Combinatorial Optimisation\" (CP 2025, LIPIcs.CP.2025.31, github: SeppiaBrilla/EFE_project)** ⭐⭐⭐⭐\nTransformer encoder 直接从 problem 高层表示学 instance feature 来选 model+solver 组合。我们 S4 AI dataset v0 在做 feature extraction，这个工作把 transformer 当 feature learner 而非 solver——完全符合我们 AI shadow-only 安全契约。\n\n**11. Jiang, Wu, Li, Cao, Zhang — \"Large Language Models as End-to-end Combinatorial Optimization Solvers\" (NeurIPS 2025, arXiv:2509.16865)** ⭐⭐\n两阶段 SFT + feasibility-and-optimality-aware RL 把 LLM 训成端到端 CO solver。借鉴度低（我们不能让 LLM 当 solver），但他们的 feasibility-aware reward design 对我们的 AI 候选排序训练有参考价值。\n\n**12. DeepMind FunSearch + 2024-12 升级版 — \"Mathematical discoveries from program search with LLMs\" (Nature 2024) + competitive programming 升级 (arXiv 2024-12)** ⭐⭐⭐\nLLM + 进化算法搜启发式程序，bin packing 任务上发现的新启发式打败传统 best-fit。我们的 candidate ordering 启发式如果固化进代码，可以让 FunSearch 风格的程序进化在 sandbox 里跑——产出仍然是 hint 而非 proof，符合契约。\n\n## 末尾推荐：最值得深入读的 3 个\n\n1. **Dekker et al. \"Towards Modern and Modular SAT for LCG\" (LIPIcs.CP.2025.42)** — 因为我们项目就是 LCG-style，他们把 IPASIR-UP 做完整接入并放出 Huub，可以直接对比 CP-SAT 在我们 ghost rectangle 上的表现差异。\n2. **Baauw et al. \"Conflict Analysis Based on Cutting-Planes for CP\" (LIPIcs.CP.2025.4)** — cutting-plane learning 对我们 binding/routing 的整数线性约束是质变级提升。\n3. **Shi et al. \"DynamicSAT\" (LIPIcs.CP.2025.34)** — 我们 S6 worker matrix 调优正好缺这个理论框架，bandit 在线切参直接对接。\n\n## Sources\n\n- [CP 2025 Accepted Papers](https://cp2025.a4cp.org/accepted_papers.html)\n- [LIPIcs Volume 340 (CP 2025)](https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/volume/LIPIcs-volume-340)\n- [CP 2024 Accepted Papers](https://cp2024.a4cp.org/accepted_papers.html)\n- [LIPIcs Volume 341 (SAT 2025)](https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/volume/LIPIcs-volume-341)\n- [SAT 2025 Papers](https://satisfiability.org/SAT25/papers/)\n- [DBLP SAT 2024](https://dblp.org/db/conf/sat/sat2024.html)\n- [CPAIOR 2024 Accepted Papers](https://sites.google.com/view/cpaior2024/accepted-papers)\n- [CPAIOR 2025 Proceedings (LNCS 15762/15763)](https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-3-031-95976-9)\n- [Baauw et al. Conflict Analysis Cutting-Planes (LIPIcs.CP.2025.4)](https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.4)\n- [Dekker et al. Modern Modular SAT for LCG (LIPIcs.CP.2025.42)](https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.42)\n- [Lin et al. PartiMIP (LIPIcs.CP.2025.26)](https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.26) + [GitHub](https://github.com/shaowei-cai-group/PartiMIP)\n- [Lewander et al. Dependency-Curated LNS (LIPIcs.CP.2025.20)](https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.20)\n- [Davies ViolationLS CPAIOR 2024](https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1007/978-3-031-60597-0_16)\n- [Shi et al. DynamicSAT (LIPIcs.CP.2025.34)](https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.34) + [GitHub](https://github.com/cure-lab/DynamicSAT)\n- [Schreiber et al. Streamlining Distributed SAT (LIPIcs.SAT.2025.27)](https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.SAT.2025.27)\n- [Pellegrino et al. Transformer Feature Learning (LIPIcs.CP.2025.31)](https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.31)\n- [LLM as End-to-end CO Solvers (arXiv 2509.16865)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16865)\n- [FunSearch — DeepMind/Nature 2024](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-06924-6)\n- [awesome-fm4co](https://github.com/ai4co/awesome-fm4co)\n- [awesome-ml4co](https://github.com/Thinklab-SJTU/awesome-ml4co)\n- [NeurIPS 2025 Papers](https://neurips.cc/virtual/2025/papers.html)\n- [IJCAI 2025 Main Track](https://2025.ijcai.org/montreal-main-track-accepted-papers/)\n- [Beck et al. Transition Dominance — Best Technical Paper CP 2025 (LIPIcs.CP.2025.5)](https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.5)"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5211,"cache_read_input_tokens":54510,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5211,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"}},"requestId":"req_011Caors28G896oRgAPjnSNh","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"a01de30a-27df-42df-93ff-58925ad03b7f","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:45:22.186Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
